opt.org/\"><code>scip</code></a> </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\">  </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\">  </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\">  </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> XPRESS* </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> <a href=\"https://www.fico.com/en/products/fico-xpress-optimization\"><code>xpress</code></a> </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\">  </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\">  </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\">  </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;background-color: rgba(236, 236, 236, 1) !important;\"> ✓ </td>\n  </tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"solver-table-note\">\n<p>* Commercial solver; free academic licenses are available.</p>\n<p>QP: solvers with a quadratic-objective interface receive the objective directly; the other conic solvers solve a second-order-cone reformulation.</p>\n<p>Without a commercial solver, mixed-integer problems can use HIGHS or GLPK_MI (MILP only), SCIP (MILP, MIQP, MISOCP) or ECOS_BB.</p>\n<p>ECOS_BB is used only when named, as in psolve(prob, solver = \"ECOS_BB\").</p>\n<p>Smooth nonlinear programs, psolve(prob, nlp = TRUE), use the separate NLP solvers Uno or IPOPT; see Using Other Solvers.</p>\n</div>\n\n`````\n:::\n:::\n\n\nFor more detail, see [Using Other Solvers](examples/solvers/using-other-solvers.qmd).\n\n## News\n\nFor the latest news, please see the [Package\nDocumentation](https://www.cvxgrp.org/CVXR).\n\n`CVXR` continues to be developed on\n[Github](https://github.com/cvxgrp/CVXR). The 1.9.3 release fixes further\nwrong answers, makes matrix norms agree with base R, and chooses solvers as\nCVXPY 1.9 does; the 1.9.2 release fixes correctness issues, overhauls solver\noptions, and speeds up solves; the 1.9.1 release adds disciplined nonlinear\nprogramming (DNLP); and the 1.8.x series was a major release implementing a\ncomplete rewrite using R's S7 class system, making `CVXR` isomorphic with\nCVXPY and adding support for DGP, DPP, and DQCP. More details can be found in [What's New](whatsnew.qmd).\n\n## Installing `CVXR`\n\n`CVXR` is installed like any other R package from\n[CRAN](https://cloud.r-project.org).\n\n```r\ninstall.packages(\"CVXR\")\n```\n\n## Documentation\n\nAn introductory vignette is installed with the package and can be\nviewed using `vignette(\"cvxr_intro\", package = \"CVXR\")`.\n\nA full [hands-on tutorial book](https://bnaras.github.io/cvxr_tutorial/),\n*Convex Optimization in R Using CVXR* (prepared for useR! 2026), walks\nthrough the package step by step---from your first model through DCP,\nregression, portfolio optimization, and mixed-integer and nonlinear\nproblems---with worked examples and exercises in every chapter.\n\nA large set of examples, many ported from `CVXPY`, are available on\nthis site:\n\n- [Quick Introduction](examples/intro.qmd) — a five-minute overview\n- [Longer Introduction](examples/gentle-intro.qmd) — more detailed walkthrough\n- Tutorial examples organized by topic in the sidebar\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nWe are grateful to [Steven\nDiamond](https://stevendiamond.me), [John Miller](https://millerjohnp.github.io/), and Paul\nKunsberg Rosenfield for their contributions to the software's\ndevelopment. In particular, we are indebted to Steven Diamond for his\nwork on [CVXPY](http://www.cvxpy.org). Most of `CVXR`'s\ncode, documentation, and examples were ported from the Python library.\n\n## About this site\n\nThis site is built with [Quarto](https://quarto.org) and is\nre-generated with every new release of `CVXR`. Many\n[CVXPY](https://cvxpy.org) examples have been directly converted to\n`CVXR`, serving both as tests of the package and as illustrations of\nits use.\n\n## References\n",
